# Local-only folders (not pushed to repo).
# LEADING SLASH IS LOAD-BEARING (S17, found by CI): an unanchored `Prompts/` matches a
# directory of that name at ANY depth, and on Windows the match is case-insensitive — so
# it also swallowed packages/core/src/medcore/prompts/. The entire safety policy (refusal
# rules, injection defence, citation requirement) was therefore never in version control,
# and a fresh clone could not start: `prompt system_v1 not found; available: []`.
# Silent, because the files existed locally and every local run passed.
/demo/
.terraform/
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
/Documents/

# --- Node / Next.js (S10.3) ---
# Every rule below is written with its match-depth INTENDED, not copied from a template.
# S17-A1: `Prompts/` (no leading slash) matched a directory of that name at ANY depth and
# silently excluded packages/core/src/medcore/prompts/ — the safety policy never reached git.
# So: unanchored here means "at any depth, deliberately", because these are generated in
# every app directory. Anything that must match only at the repo root gets a leading slash.
# CAUGHT WHILE WRITING THIS BLOCK: git only treats `#` as a comment when it is the FIRST
# character of a line. `node_modules/   # note` is a pattern that literally matches a path
# containing that text, so the rule silently does nothing — same failure shape as the
# python-dotenv `VAR=  # note` finding in I.1. Verified empirically: with trailing comments
# `git check-ignore apps/web/node_modules/probe.js` reported NOT ignored.
#
# Unanchored below = "at any depth", deliberately: these are generated in every app dir.
# Anything that must match only at the repo root gets a leading slash.
node_modules/
.next/
out/
next-env.d.ts
.turbo/
*.tsbuildinfo

# Playwright (S10.6b)
test-results/
playwright-report/
